# Common Completion and Knowledge Classification

Read this when approaching review or completion, and before any gate or Git action.

Every task MUST compare the final diff, the requirements, the check results and the Living Spec impact before reporting completion. Before reporting completion or integration, classify the Living Spec impact:

- Authorized contract changes go to capabilities/stories.
- Structural changes go to architecture.
- Architecture decisions and lasting policy changes that override a MUST go to ADRs.
- Existing policy changes go to rules/playbooks.
- New general rules and reusable procedures are optional promotions: draft first and request only decisions beyond existing authorization.
- A no-impact change needs a reason, not a new document.

## Policy

Read `devkit spec policy` before deciding gate or Git actions.

- The main boundaries are `plan-accept`, `proposal-handoff` and `dev-integration`, each independently `ask|auto`.
- An `ask` boundary needs authority for its exact effects; reuse an explicit decision already given instead of asking again.
- `auto` delegates that boundary to the Host after readiness checks; report policy as the authority, never as a human approval.
- Auto requires the owning base and prospective policy to allow it; source-only policy edits cannot authorize themselves.
- Existing task authorization satisfies `plan-intent` without a fourth repeated approval.
- `accept_only` settles proposal direction and leaves Development for later.
- `accept_and_handoff` can settle both boundaries in one interaction: ask only for the boundaries whose policy is `ask`, and disclose each effect.
- A stale source/report invalidates the old request; inspect partial state and authorize only remaining effects. Preserve exact source SHA, report URI, authority and recovery state.
- Planning integration is part of `plan-accept`, not another `dev-integration` decision; that boundary governs the final Development result.
- Neither mode bypasses readiness, host restrictions or publication policy.

For the final result:

- `[GATE_POLICY: dev-integration policy=ask]`: present the verified result, then obtain the user's OK:
{% if execution.environment == "local" %}  - Local MCP integration: call `integration_integrate` directly. Its native confirmation is the user's OK for this gate; it is bound to the verified source commit, the confirmation target and (within that call) its descendants, and the merge/cleanup scope. Ask no separate chat approval and do not require `integration_prepare` first.
  - A waiting `gate:dev-integration` permits this call; do not assert `--given dev-integration` before native acceptance.
  - CLI fallback: a conversational OK unless already authorized. A PR: its ready state, to which a runner binds the gate.
  - Do not write the OK to a file (`ADR-integration-approval-asserted`). For a spec bundle, pass `--given dev-integration` to each `devkit workflow next --unit <dir>` after it. Name the OK (`mcp-elicitation` when confirmed through `integration_integrate`) as the authority in the integration report.
{% else %}  - Explicit CLI route: a conversational OK unless already authorized. A PR: its ready state, to which a runner binds the gate.
  - Do not write the OK to a file (`ADR-integration-approval-asserted`). For a spec bundle, pass `--given dev-integration` to each `devkit workflow next --unit <dir>` after it. Name the OK as the authority in the integration report.
{% endif %}  - On an objection, take the stage's return path (`devkit workflow return --unit <dir> --to review-refine --note "..."`); an objection about direction is a close ([classification](classification.md) §4).
- `policy=auto`: once review, reconciliation and required checks pass, at every effort, the policy delegates the gate. `devkit workflow next` lists `[GATE_PASSED: dev-integration ... source=policy]` (JSON `passed_gates`), the basis the integration report cites. Do not describe policy delegation as a human approval or review.
{% if execution.environment == "local" %}- Shared base `policy=auto` also authorizes verified local merge and exact task cleanup through `integration_integrate`, reporting `authority: policy` without elicitation.
  - The Host invokes it after the final report; prepare alone grants no authority.
  - Source-only auto cannot authorize its own merge.
  - Auto PR execution and cloud execution/resume refuse.
  - Prospective auto-to-ask revocation refuses before mutation; remaining cleanup requires unchanged auto policy.
- Neither value grants commit or publication authority or overrides host permissions.
  - After a host refusal, do not switch execution routes to evade it.
  - Without MCP, auto has no automatic CLI substitute; manual execution requires separate authority.
{% else %}- Shared base `policy=auto` grants no cloud execution, resume or publication authority.
  - There is no automatic CLI substitute: return verified results to the Host unless a separate explicit CLI/PR authorization exists.
  - Neither policy value overrides host permissions; after a refusal, do not reroute.
{% endif %}- `GIT_POLICY` (worktree, pull request and branch cleanup): read [git policy](git-policy.md) and decide before the first edit.

## Reconciliation record

- By PR preparation, create the spec's `reconcile.md` with `reconcile_id`, `status: pending|resolved`, and `targets` (`file`, `resolution: modified|waived`, `rationale` for a waiver).
- Set resolved only after every target is reflected in the comparison diff or has a waiver of at least 20 non-whitespace Unicode characters. Required contract mismatches cannot be waived.
- For empty targets, give no-impact reasoning in the body.
- After integration, keep resolved records in place and mark the spec completed.

## Checks and commits

- `git commit` runs the commit gate on the index (`devkit rules check --staged` reproduces it); `devkit rules check --ci --base <ref>` checks merge-base through HEAD (default `origin/main`).
- The rules check structure and form, and require each `modified` target of a resolved record to be changed on the branch; they do not prove semantic impact.
- The commit gate counts the staged diff together with the branch's earlier commits since its merge base, so a target an earlier commit changed (the ADR the handoff promoted) needs no second edit.
- Intermediate commits pass the same gate; a `pending` reconcile record is allowed while the spec is not `completed`.
- An intermediate commit grants no merge authority and is never final completion evidence: the final cumulative check runs on the integrated head, and `dev-integration` still requires the Living Spec reflection and the resolved and completed states.

{% if execution.environment == "local" %}- Before the native approval, prepare the task branch against the base, re-verify changed paths and run the cumulative check; this changes no base ref and cleans nothing up.
- After `dev-integration`, the `integrate` stage (`spec-integrate`) integrates the base branch, re-verifies only what conflict resolution changed, confirms the reconcile record and runs the cumulative check; it does not restart code review and adds no workflow gate.
- Delegated support roles return the fields in [envelope](envelope.md) and never change the index or HEAD.
{% else %}- Before integration approval, prepare the task branch against the base, re-verify changed paths and run the cumulative check; this changes no base ref and cleans nothing up.
- After `dev-integration`, the `integrate` stage (`spec-integrate`) integrates the base branch, re-verifies only what conflict resolution changed, confirms the reconcile record and runs the cumulative check; it does not restart code review and adds no workflow gate.
- Delegated support roles return the fields in [envelope](envelope.md) and never change the index or HEAD.
{% endif %}- Reconcile verification is a `reconcile-check` envelope at `living-spec-draft` at every effort ([effort](effort.md), [review loop](review-loop.md)), naming who verified, the evidence and any omission reason; fix findings and write a new check.
- After review fixes or changed inputs, recheck impact; for new targets, return `reconcile.md` to pending.
- Review final docs and governance changes too.
- Never automatically delete a dirty worktree.
- Report implementation complete, committed, integrated and session ended as distinct facts.

`session` depth (session: no records):

- Run the checks and the effort's review.
- Commit with a plain `git commit` before `dev-integration` (the approval covers committed work; a PR is pushed then), without `spec-integrate`.
- Exception: on the base branch with no PR under `dev-integration = ask`, start `dev-<slug>`, or get the approval before committing ([git policy](git-policy.md)).
- Work the user's authorized scope; do not silently abandon remaining work for a session time limit.

## Source feedback resolution

- Only `open` feedback is actionable.
- `promoted` is terminal once handed to identified follow-up work; progress belongs to that destination, and
  finishing it MUST NOT convert the source to `resolved`.
- `resolved` is terminal for a directly addressed and verified observation without promotion, in either a session
  or a spec task.
- Terminal source references remain traceability: they require no completion disposition, re-verification or edits.

Before reporting completion, review only explicitly linked open sources addressed directly by the task against
the final diff and verification. For a spec, record their outcomes in `reconcile.md` at `living-spec-draft`; for a
session, do this at `report-session` before its commit and report outcomes without creating a new artifact.
This obligation applies even with requested-only capture or `collect-feedback` off.

- Change an open direct-fix source to `resolved` only when the authorized implementation fully addresses it and
  applicable tests/review verify the result. Append a Disposition naming the SPEC ID (or changed session paths),
  implemented outcome and concrete verification evidence.
- Do not resolve unrelated feedback found by a sweep; it is not authorized.
- Keep partial, deferred or unverified direct fixes open; report remaining scope and destination. Required work
  still in the agreed scope cannot be deferred to claim completion.
- Handoff, planning, document checks or integration alone are not fix verification. A branch resolution does not
  assert integration; the implementation and FB update reach the base through normal integration.
- If later changes invalidate the evidence of a resolution made by this task, restore that source to open and
  explain remaining work before completion. Do not revisit pre-existing terminal sources.
- Capture never changes status.
- An authorized direct fix needs no separate hygiene session.
- Only open sources requiring updates belong in delegated write scope; the Host handles an out-of-boundary
  disposition.

## Deferred Work Handoff

Before deleting a source proposal or reporting completion, find concrete work its roadmap or the final scope explicitly retains for later.

1. Each such follow-up MUST be tracked: by default as feedback for Backlog (`/spec-feedback`), or as a draft proposal when concrete enough. For a draft, link or update a matching open `PROP-...` (`devkit spec search "<topic>" --kind proposal`), else run `devkit spec proposal new <slug>`.
2. Record the source, remaining scope, reason for deferral, dependencies or restart conditions, and expected acceptance checks; group work with the same outcome.
3. Reference the destination `FB-...` or `PROP-...` by full ID from the source `reconcile.md` (or a `session` task's final report). If the original proposal is removed, reference the retained source spec or Git history from the proposal. A future-work paragraph in a capability document is no handoff.
4. Verify the destination exists (`devkit spec list feedback` or `devkit spec list proposal`) before claiming it is tracked.

- Drafting a follow-up neither accepts it nor authorizes implementation.
- Required acceptance criteria or defects still in the agreed scope MUST remain open; never move them to a proposal to claim completion.
- The handoff is part of completion in every route.

